    { "type": "header", "content": "Chapter 1 — Understanding dialysis" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "ch1_eyebrow", "label": "Eyebrow", "default": "Chapter 01" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "ch1_heading", "label": "Heading", "default": "Understanding dialysis" },
    { "type": "richtext", "id": "ch1_body", "label": "Body", "default": "<p>When your kidneys can no longer filter your blood well enough on their own, dialysis does that work for them — removing waste, extra fluid, and balancing minerals. It isn't a cure, but for millions of people it is a steady, manageable part of daily life.</p><p><strong>Two main types:</strong> <em>Haemodialysis (HD)</em> filters your blood through a machine, usually at a centre three times a week. <em>Peritoneal dialysis (PD)</em> uses the lining of your abdomen and is often done at home. Your care team will help you choose what fits your body and your life.</p>" },
    { "type": "image_picker", "id": "ch1_image", "label": "Image" },

